:: Diner Suggestion [event main_quest] {"position":"3150,500","size":"100,100"}
The neon "Open" sign outside $diner.name buzzes like it always does, throwing its sickly yellow light over the cracked sidewalk. I push through the glass door and the smell hits me before the air conditioning does: grease, burnt coffee and fryer oil old enough to vote. It's mid-afternoon, the lunch crowd is gone and the dinner rush hasn't started, so the place is empty except for a couple of regulars nursing bottomless cups at the counter.

I clock in at the back, tie on my apron and wait for a quiet moment. When $manager.callName disappears into his office, I follow him.

<<linkreplace "Approach $manager.callName">>
The office is barely bigger than a closet. Order pads and old invoices are stacked on every flat surface. $manager.callName sits in the middle of it with his sleeves rolled up, glaring at a pile of receipts like they owe him money.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "Hey, got a minute?" },
{ who: $manager, text: "If this is about switching shifts again, the answer's no. We're already short." },
{ who: $player, text: "It's not about shifts. It's something that could actually help the diner." },
{ who: $manager, text: "Huh. That's a new one." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

<<linkreplace "Explain the situation">>
He leans back in his creaky chair and folds his arms, which is about as much attention as anyone ever gets from him.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "You know the startup thing you like to make fun of? $company.prototype.name? It's a little box that picks up on how a room feels, whether people are bored or stressed or comfortable, and then it nudges things so they settle in a bit. And people who settle in stay longer and order more. That's the theory. I just can't get anybody to let me prove it." },
{ who: $manager, text: "And you're coming to me because...?" },
{ who: $player, text: "Because I work here and I know the place. I'd run it during my own shift, and it wouldn't cost you a dime. If it doesn't do anything, I drop it and you never hear about it again." },
{ who: $manager, text: "Sounds like a fancy way of saying you want to play with your gadget in my diner. What if customers get weirded out?" },
{ who: $player, text: "They won't even see it. It sits behind the menus by the register and hums, that's all. Honestly, you'll forget it's there." },
{ who: $manager, text: "Kid, I don't forget anything that happens in this building. That's the job." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

<<linkreplace "Sweeten the deal">>
$manager.callName rubs his jaw. He isn't buying it, so I try the one thing I was hoping I wouldn't need.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "Okay, there's something else. My $sister.relation's been looking for a part-time job. She's reliable, she's great with people, and she already said she'd be happy to wait tables here, so you'd get an extra pair of hands without having to train some stranger from scratch." },
{ who: $manager, text: "Your $sister.relation? The one who comes in sometimes and steals your fries?" },
{ who: $player, text: "That's her. She means it, she wants to help out at home. Let me run the test, and she starts whenever you need her." },
{ who: $manager, text: "Extra hands never hurt, especially on weekends..." },
{ text: "He squints at me over the receipts." },
{ who: $manager, text: "You really think that toy of yours brings in money?" },
{ who: $player, text: "I've tried it on a small scale, and yeah, I think it does. Give me one shift. If it doesn't help, I drop it, and she still gets the job." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

<<linkreplace "Let him think it over">>
He stares at me for a long moment, tapping a pen against the desk. The kitchen leaks in through the door: plates clattering, the fryer hissing, somebody yelling that table four wants extra pickles. Finally he sighs like I've personally ruined his afternoon.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $manager, text: "Alright. One shift. You run your thing during your next morning shift, and the second a customer looks confused or complains, it goes off. And your $sister.relation starts this weekend. No funny business." },
{ who: $player, text: "Deal. I really don't think you'll regret it." },
{ who: $manager, text: "I regret most things, kid. Now get back out there before the dinner rush hits." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

I step out of the office and tie my apron a little tighter. It's one shift in a greasy diner, nothing anyone would ever write an article about, but it's the first yes I've heard all week. And $sister.callName is going to be working three feet from my counter every weekend, which I'm not exactly going to complain about.

The grill hisses as the first early dinner orders come in. I grab the coffee pot and get back to work, already picking the spot where the prototype can see every booth.

